invariant: INV-49
file: sha512_avx2.c
catches: test/docker-aes-runtime-qemu.sh sha512-avx2
reason: sums, the array sha512_avx2_compress_blocks stores four sums at a
  time to, loses its declared alignment of 32 bytes. gcc 13.3 still
  aligns the function's frame to 32 bytes and then places the array 16
  bytes past a multiple of 32, so two of every four stores lie across a
  cache line, and one across a page boundary where the frame meets one.
  clang 18 and 23 compile the same instructions with the declaration and
  without, so make lint-frame-align, which reads clang's code, passes.
  Every digest is right. bin/sha2_equiv_test finds the sixteen sums in
  the stack the kernel leaves, for a caller at each of two stack
  positions 16 bytes apart, and requires each four at a multiple of 32
  bytes. The lane builds with gcc for x86-64 and runs under qemu-x86_64,
  so the verdict is the same on every machine. It skips without docker,
  and this then reports unguarded rather than caught.

--- old
    _Alignas(32) uint64_t sums[16];
--- new
    uint64_t sums[16];
